That test is messy. Let me write a clean reproduction. The core idea: a function ref that triggers a state update when called with `null` (detach), and the Slot creating a new composed ref each render → loop.

Let me simplify. The mechanism I want to test:
- Parent component with state that re-renders on some event (like typing).
- Child function ref that, when called with `null` (detached in React 19), schedules a state update.
- Slot composes the ref each render (new identity).

React 19 will detach the old ref (call with null) and attach the new ref whenever the ref callback identity changes between renders. If the null-call triggers a state update → loop.

But careful: a component that re-renders (e.g., typing in an input) will re-create the composed ref each render. React 19 will then detach/attach. If the child's function ref schedules state on detach (null call), it loops.

Let me write a clean test:

```tsx
function Repro() {
  const [, setTick] = useState(0);
  const ref = useCallback((node) => {
    // plate/slate style ref
    if (node === null) {
      setTick((n) => n + 1); // schedule update on detach
    }
  }, []);
  return <Slot ref={ref}><button>hi</button></Slot>;
}
```

On mount: composed ref = composeRefs(ref, undefined). `ref(button)` — node !== null, no setTick. Good.
Then some re-render happens. How to trigger re-render? Type in an input. Let me make the component include an input and typing triggers re-render:

Actually simpler: trigger re-render via userEvent on the button that calls setState. Let me make a self-contained repro:

```tsx
function Repro() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  const ref = useCallback((node) => {
    if (node === null) {
      setText((t) => t + '!'); // schedules update on detach
    }
  }, []);
  return (
    <div>
      <input value={text} onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)} />
      <Slot ref={ref}><button>hi</button></Slot>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Type 'a' → setText('a') → re-render → new composed ref → React 19 detaches old ref → calls ref(null) → setText('a!') → re-render → new composed ref → detach → ref(null) → setText... loop → "Maximum update depth exceeded".

Wait, but will `setText` inside the ref callback during commit cause an error? React detects nested updates in commit phase and throws "Maximum update depth exceeded". 

Let me write this cleanly and verify the current code fails, then verify the fix makes it pass.

Actually, I need to be careful: when does React 19 call the old ref callback with null on identity change? Yes — in React 19, if a ref callback changes identity between renders, React detaches the previous ref by calling it with `null` (or calling its cleanup), then attaches the new one. This is documented behavior.

But wait, there's a subtlety — will React call the old ref with null during the commit, then during the SAME commit the new ref? If the ref null-call triggers setText, React schedules a new render. During that new render's commit, the composed ref identity changed AGAIN (because setText changed the input value, re-render happens, new composed ref created), so it detaches again → loop. Yes.

Let me also double check: does the loop require the ref to schedule state ONLY on null? If it schedules on any call, then even the initial attach `ref(button)` would schedule, but attach happens during mount before any re-render — that's fine, it just triggers one re-render, then... actually on mount, `ref(button)` → setText → re-render → new composed ref → React detaches old (calls ref(null)) → setText → ... loop too. So even simpler: ref schedules on EVERY call.

Let me write the clean test now.

